<!-- ============ 4. FOLDER STRUCTURE ============ -->
<h1>4. Project Folder Structure</h1>
<p>One of the important requirements of this lab was to organize the project files into a clean, professional structure. I created separate folders for CSS and images instead of putting everything in one place. Below is the final folder layout of my project:</p>

<div class="code-block">portfolio/
&#9500;&#9472;&#9472; index.html
&#9500;&#9472;&#9472; hobbies.html
&#9500;&#9472;&#9472; contact.html
&#9500;&#9472;&#9472; gallery.html
&#9500;&#9472;&#9472; skills.html
&#9500;&#9472;&#9472; css/
&#9474;   &#9492;&#9472;&#9472; style.css
&#9500;&#9472;&#9472; images/
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; 1.jpg
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; 2.jpg
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; 3.jpg
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; 4.jpg
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; Passport-Size-Pic.jpg
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; MyPic.jpg
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; Hobby.jpg
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; Pic1.jpeg
&#9474;   &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; Pic2.jpeg
&#9474;   &#9492;&#9472;&#9472; Pic3.jpeg
&#9492;&#9472;&#9472; README.md</div>

<p>Every HTML page links to the stylesheet using the relative path <strong>css/style.css</strong>, and all image references use the <strong>images/</strong> prefix. This approach keeps everything neat and avoids the common mistake of having scattered files in the root directory.</p>


<!-- ============ 5. PAGE DESCRIPTIONS ============ -->
<h1>5. Detailed Page Descriptions</h1>

<h2>5a. Home Page (index.html)</h2>
<p>The home page is the main landing page of my portfolio. At the top, there is a header section that displays my passport-sized profile picture on the left and my name along with a short tagline on the right. I used the CSS <strong>float: left</strong> property on the profile picture so that the text wraps beside it naturally, and then I used a <strong>clear</strong> div to reset the flow after the header.</p>

<p>Below the header, there is a horizontal navigation bar that links to all five pages. The navigation is styled with inline-block display so the links sit in a single row. The currently active page is highlighted with a bottom border to let the visitor know where they are on the site.</p>

<p>The main content of the home page is the "About Me" section. Here I have used a float layout where the descriptive text is floated to the left (taking up about 60% of the width) and an image is floated to the right (taking up 36%). This creates a professional side-by-side arrangement without using flexbox or grid. After that, I have separate sections for My Background, Academic Journey, and Current Goals.</p>

<p>This page uses <strong>1 image</strong> from my newly added collection.</p>


<h2>5b. Hobbies Page (hobbies.html)</h2>
<p>The hobbies page lists the activities I enjoy doing when I am not busy with my university assignments. It starts with a centered page header and the same navigation bar as the home page.</p>

<p>The hobbies are displayed as four cards arranged in a two-column layout. I used <strong>float: left</strong> on each card with a width of 47% and a 6% right margin on the odd-numbered cards. After every two cards, I placed a <strong>&lt;div class="clear"&gt;</strong> to break the float and start a new row. Each card has a numbered badge, a heading, and a short paragraph describing the hobby.</p>

<p>Below the cards, there is a "Captured Moments" section that shows a relevant image from my outdoor activities. This image is displayed using a float-based layout as well.</p>

<p>This page uses <strong>1 image</strong> from my newly added collection (the football sunset photo).</p>


<h2>5c. Skills Page (skills.html)</h2>
<p>The skills page is one of the more content-rich pages of my portfolio. It has three main sections.</p>

<p>The first section is a <strong>Programming Skills</strong> table that lists the languages and tools I have worked with, along with my proficiency level and where I have used them. The table is styled with a dark header row and alternating background colours on the data rows to improve readability.</p>

<p>The second section is called <strong>"Skills in Action"</strong> and it displays three images arranged in a row using <strong>float: left</strong>. Each image takes up roughly 30% of the width with some margin in between. A clear div at the end resets the float. Each image has a small caption below it.</p>

<p>The third section presents my <strong>Spoken Languages</strong> in three cards (Urdu, English, Arabic), also arranged using <strong>float: left</strong> with each card taking 30% width. Finally, there is a <strong>Soft Skills</strong> section where I have used a float layout with a bulleted list on the left and an image on the right.</p>

<p>This page uses <strong>3 images</strong> from my newly added collection.</p>


<h2>5d. Image Gallery Page (gallery.html)</h2>
<p>The gallery page is a dedicated photo gallery that displays all of my images in an organized grid format. This is where I have used the float and clear properties most extensively.</p>

<p>The images are arranged in rows of three. Each gallery item is set to <strong>float: left</strong> with a width of 30% and a 5% right margin. Every third item has its right margin removed, and the item following it (i.e., the first item of the next row) has <strong>clear: left</strong> applied to force a new row. After each row of three, a clear div is also placed for extra safety.</p>

<p>Each image has a short caption below it that describes what is happening in the photo. On hover, the image border colour changes slightly to give the user a visual feedback. The gallery currently contains <strong>9 images</strong> spread across 3 rows, including all my newly added photos and the ones from my travels.</p>


<h2>5e. Contact Page (contact.html)</h2>
<p>The contact page has all my contact information and a simple message form. It begins with a table that lists my full name, email address, phone number, university, city, and my GitHub profile link. The table has a label column on the left (with a light background) and a data column on the right.</p>

<p>Below the table, there is a section with two images displayed side by side using <strong>float: left</strong>, each with a small caption underneath. After the images, I have placed a contact form with fields for the visitor's name, email, subject, and message, along with a "Send Message" submit button. The form is styled to match the rest of the website's colour scheme.</p>

<p>This page uses <strong>2 images</strong> from my newly added collection.</p>


<!-- ============ 6. CSS APPROACH ============ -->
<h1>6. CSS Styling Approach</h1>
<p>The biggest change from Lab 2 to Lab 3 was moving all the CSS into one external file. In Lab 2, I had written separate &lt;style&gt; blocks inside each HTML page, which led to a lot of duplicated code and inconsistency. Some pages had a greenish navigation bar, others had an olive-coloured one, and the fonts were different on the contact page compared to the rest.</p>

<p>In this lab, I created a single <strong>css/style.css</strong> file and linked it in the &lt;head&gt; of every HTML page using:</p>

<div class="code-block">&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="css/style.css"&gt;</div>

<p>The key design decisions I made were:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Colour Palette:</strong> I chose a warm, earthy set of colours &ndash; off-white backgrounds (#f5f4f0), beige header tones (#e2ded3), and olive green accents (#5c6b4e, #9aab82). These colours feel natural and clean without being flashy.</li>
    <li><strong>Typography:</strong> I used Arial and Helvetica as the primary fonts because they are simple, readable, and widely available on all systems.</li>
    <li><strong>Consistency:</strong> Every page shares the same header style, navigation bar, content section cards, and footer. This gives the portfolio a unified look throughout.</li>
    <li><strong>Hover Effects:</strong> I added subtle hover transitions on navigation links (bottom border appears), buttons (darker background), and image borders (colour change) to make the site feel interactive.</li>
</ul>


<!-- ============ 7. FLOAT AND CLEAR ============ -->
<h1>7. Use of Float and Clear</h1>
<p>Using CSS float and clear was a core requirement of this lab, and I have applied them on every single page. Here is a summary of where and how I used these properties:</p>

<table class="doc-table">
    <tr>
        <th>Page</th>
        <th>Where Float is Used</th>
        <th>How Clear is Applied</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Home</td>
        <td>Profile picture floated left in header; text block floated left and image floated right in About Me section</td>
        <td>&lt;div class="clear"&gt; after header and after float-row</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Hobbies</td>
        <td>Hobby cards floated left in 2-column layout; hobby image floated left</td>
        <td>&lt;div class="clear"&gt; after every 2 cards and after image section</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Skills</td>
        <td>Skill images floated left in a row; language cards floated left in 3 columns; soft skills text floated left with image floated right</td>
        <td>&lt;div class="clear"&gt; after image row, language cards, and soft skills section</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Gallery</td>
        <td>All gallery items floated left with 30% width, 3 per row</td>
        <td>&lt;div class="clear"&gt; after every row of 3; also clear:left on every 3n+1 item via CSS</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Contact</td>
        <td>Contact images floated left in a row</td>
        <td>&lt;div class="clear"&gt; after the image row</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<p>I also used <strong>overflow: hidden</strong> on the parent containers of floated elements. This is a common clearfix technique that ensures the parent container properly wraps around its floated children without collapsing.</p>

<div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>Note:</strong> I deliberately avoided using CSS Flexbox or Grid for the main layouts because the lab specifically required float and clear. However, I understand that in modern web development, Flexbox and Grid are generally preferred for layout work.
</div>


<!-- ============ 8. GIT ============ -->
<h1>8. Version Control with Git</h1>
<p>For this lab, I used Git to track all changes to my project and pushed the code to a GitHub repository. The steps I followed were:</p>

<ol>
    <li>Opened the terminal in VS Code and navigated to my project folder.</li>
    <li>Ran <strong>git init</strong> to initialize a new Git repository in the project directory.</li>
    <li>Used <strong>git add .</strong> to stage all the files (HTML pages, CSS, images, and README).</li>
    <li>Committed the changes with <strong>git commit -m "Lab 3: Added external CSS, float layouts, organized folders"</strong>.</li>
    <li>Created a new repository on GitHub and linked it using <strong>git remote add origin</strong>.</li>
    <li>Pushed everything to GitHub using <strong>git push -u origin main</strong>.</li>
</ol>

<p>All of the project files &ndash; including the five HTML pages, the css/ folder with style.css, the images/ folder with all 10 images, and the README.md &ndash; are now available on my public GitHub repository.</p>

<p><strong>GitHub Repository Link:</strong> <em>https://github.com/AbdullahRana11/portfolio</em></p>


<!-- ============ 9. DEPLOYMENT ============ -->
<h1>9. Deployment via GitHub Pages</h1>
<p>After pushing the code to GitHub, I enabled GitHub Pages from the repository settings. The steps I followed were:</p>

<ol>
    <li>Went to my repository on GitHub and clicked on <strong>Settings</strong>.</li>
    <li>Scrolled down to the <strong>Pages</strong> section in the sidebar.</li>
    <li>Under "Source", I selected the <strong>main</strong> branch and the <strong>/ (root)</strong> folder.</li>
    <li>Clicked <strong>Save</strong>, and within a couple of minutes, my portfolio was live on the internet.</li>
</ol>

<p>The website is now accessible to anyone with the link, and any future changes pushed to the main branch will automatically update the live site.</p>

<p><strong>Live Portfolio Link:</strong> <em>https://abdullahrana11.github.io/portfolio/</em></p>


<!-- ============ 10. CHALLENGES ============ -->
<h1>10. Challenges Faced and How I Solved Them</h1>

<h2>Challenge 1: Float Collapsing</h2>
<p>When I first started using float on the hobby cards, the parent container was collapsing and not wrapping around the cards properly. The cards were overflowing outside their container, and the next section was overlapping with them. I solved this by adding <strong>overflow: hidden</strong> to the parent container and placing <strong>&lt;div class="clear"&gt;</strong> elements after each row of floated items.</p>

<h2>Challenge 2: Keeping Consistent Styling</h2>
<p>In Lab 2, each page had its own style block and the colours were slightly different everywhere. When I moved everything to one file, I had to go through all the pages carefully and remove the old &lt;style&gt; blocks. Then I re-structured the CSS with organized sections (header, nav, content, gallery, etc.) so everything was clean and easy to maintain.</p>

<h2>Challenge 3: Image Sizing in Gallery</h2>
<p>Some of my images were portrait-oriented and some were landscape, so they looked uneven in the gallery grid. I fixed this by using <strong>object-fit: cover</strong> on all gallery images with a fixed height of 200px. This crops the images to fill the space uniformly while keeping the layout aligned.</p>

<h2>Challenge 4: Navigation Active State</h2>
<p>I wanted to show which page the user is currently on by highlighting the corresponding navigation link. Since I am not using JavaScript, I manually added a class <strong>class="active"</strong> to the correct link on each page. It is a simple approach but it works well and clearly indicates the current page to the user.</p>


<!-- ============ 11. WHAT I LEARNED ============ -->
<h1>11. What I Learned</h1>
<p>This lab was a very practical and hands-on learning experience for me. Here are the main things I learned:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>External CSS is much better than internal CSS.</strong> Having a single stylesheet for the whole site saves time, reduces errors, and ensures that everything looks the same. If I need to change the navigation colour, I only change it in one place and it updates on all pages.</li>

    <li><strong>Float and clear are old but important concepts.</strong> Even though modern CSS has Flexbox and Grid, understanding how float works is valuable because a lot of existing websites and legacy code still use it. I now understand how float removes elements from the normal document flow and why clearing is necessary.</li>

    <li><strong>File organization matters.</strong> Keeping HTML in the root, CSS in a css/ folder, and images in an images/ folder makes the project much easier to navigate. When the project grows bigger, this structure becomes essential.</li>

    <li><strong>Git is a powerful tool.</strong> Being able to track changes, commit progress, and push code to a remote repository gives me confidence that my work is safe and versioned properly. I can always go back to an earlier version if something breaks.</li>

    <li><strong>Deploying with GitHub Pages is surprisingly simple.</strong> I was expecting the deployment process to be complicated, but it turned out to be just a few clicks. Having my portfolio live on the internet feels very rewarding and motivating.</li>
</ul>


<!-- ============ 12. CONCLUSION ============ -->
<h1>12. Conclusion</h1>
<p>Lab 3 was a significant step forward from the basic HTML work I did in Lab 2. This time, I did not just write HTML pages &ndash; I actually thought about the project as a whole. I considered how the files should be organized, how the design should be consistent, and how to use CSS properties like float and clear to arrange content properly on the page.</p>

<p>My portfolio now has a professional-looking structure with an external stylesheet, proper folder organization, a working image gallery, float-based layouts on every page, and a live deployment on GitHub Pages. I believe this lab has given me a strong foundation in front-end web development, and I am looking forward to learning more advanced topics like responsive design, JavaScript, and eventually back-end development in the upcoming labs.</p>

<p>I would like to thank Dr. Muhammad Khuram Shahzad and Engr. Ayesha Asif for their guidance and support throughout this lab. The instructions were clear and the lab structure helped me learn in a very systematic way.</p>

